# GeneralStaff project registry — example schema (Phase 1)
#
# Real registry lives at projects.yaml (gitignored — contains absolute
# paths). This file is the schema reference.
#
# Adding a project is a ceremony, NOT a casual edit:
#   1. Create state/<project_id>/MISSION.md in GeneralStaff
#   2. Define engineer_command (what launches the bot)
#   3. Define verification_command (independent test gate)
#   4. For user-facing work, define player_path_command (shipped-artifact gate)
#   5. Optionally define claim_battery_command (claim-vs-screen battery)
#   6. Define hands_off list (mandatory, non-empty)
#
# A project without the required fields is a slop machine. Don't register it.
#
# NOTE: GeneralStaff state lives in GeneralStaff's own directory
# under state/<project_id>/. Nothing is written to the managed
# project's working tree by the dispatcher. See PHASE-1-RESOLUTIONS
# §Q5 for rationale.
#
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Key environment variables (set in your shell, a .env loader, or the
# launcher script — NOT committed here).
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   GENERALSTAFF_REVIEWER_PROVIDER
#       Which provider runs the verification-gate reviewer. One of
#       `claude` (uses `claude -p` and your Claude subscription
#       quota), `openrouter` (paid per-token, ~$0.02/session on
#       Qwen3 Coder), or `ollama` (local, free, offline). Default:
#       `claude`. For unattended / high-volume runs, prefer
#       `openrouter` to keep pressure off your Claude quota.
#
#   GENERALSTAFF_REVIEWER_MODEL
#       Optional model override. Only meaningful when the provider
#       exposes a model knob (e.g. `qwen/qwen3-coder-plus` on
#       openrouter, `qwen3:8b` on ollama). Each provider picks a
#       sensible default when unset.
#
#   GENERALSTAFF_REVIEWER_FALLBACK_PROVIDER
#       Optional single-shot fallback. If the primary returns a
#       `[REVIEWER ERROR]`, the reviewer retries once using this
#       provider. Skipped when unset or equal to the primary.
#
#   OPENROUTER_API_KEY
#       Required when the primary or fallback provider is
#       `openrouter`. No default; if unset at launch, every cycle
#       fail-safes to `verification_failed` (by design — better a
#       loud skip than a silent pass).
#
#   OLLAMA_HOST
#       Optional. Defaults to `http://localhost:11434`. Used by the
#       ollama reviewer and its pre-flight reachability check.
#
#   GENERALSTAFF_REVIEWER_CONCURRENCY_<PROVIDER>
#       Integer, optional. Overrides the per-provider semaphore the
#       parallel session loop uses. Defaults: claude=∞, openrouter=2,
#       ollama=1. Raise on a paid tier (e.g. `=8` on OpenRouter);
#       lower to throttle. Only binds when `max_parallel_slots > 1`.
#
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Engineer provider (Phase 7, gs-270). Optional per-project.
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   engineer_provider: claude | aider | grok | codex | kimi   (optional, default: claude)
#       Which agent runs as the cycle's engineer. Default "claude" is
#       the current behavior — the dispatcher runs the project's
#       `engineer_command` string verbatim (typically a wrapper around
#       `claude -p`). Set to "aider", "grok", "codex", or "kimi" to route
#       the engineer half of the cycle to that provider — GS then generates
#       the full invocation (worktree setup + provider CLI + prompt)
#       internally and the `engineer_command` field is ignored.
#
#       Why you'd opt in: keep cycle work off your Claude subscription
#       quota.
#         - aider + OpenRouter Qwen3 Coder is ~40x cheaper per input
#           token than Claude Sonnet (pay-per-token via your OR credits).
#         - grok runs xAI's Grok CLI, billed to your flat-rate grok.com
#           subscription — no per-token cost at all. (gs-331, v0.7.1+)
#         - codex runs OpenAI's Codex CLI, billed to your ChatGPT/Codex
#           subscription via `codex login`. Experimental. (2026-07-29)
#         - kimi runs Moonshot's kimi-code CLI, billed to your Kimi Code
#           subscription via `kimi login`. Experimental. (2026-07-29)
#       Best for bulk scaffolding (types, test boilerplate, UI
#       components). Complex / algorithmic work should stay on claude.
#
#       Pre-req when set to aider:
#         - aider installed locally (`pip install aider-chat`)
#         - OPENROUTER_API_KEY in the process env (Hard Rule 8 / BYOK)
#       Pre-req when set to grok:
#         - Grok CLI installed (`curl -fsSL https://x.ai/cli/install.sh | bash`)
#         - signed in (`grok login`, writes ~/.grok/auth.json) OR a
#           GROK_DEPLOYMENT_KEY in the env (Hard Rule 8 / BYOK). No
#           per-token API key — the CLI is subscription-backed.
#       Pre-req when set to codex:
#         - Codex CLI installed (`npm install -g @openai/codex`)
#         - signed in (`codex login`). Subscription/OAuth — no API key
#           required for the ChatGPT-backed path.
#       Pre-req when set to kimi:
#         - kimi-code CLI installed (see https://www.kimi.com/code)
#         - signed in (`kimi login`). Subscription/OAuth — no API key
#           required for the managed Kimi Code path.
#
#   engineer_model: string   (optional, provider-dependent default)
#       Model override. For aider, any OpenRouter model id works (default
#       "openrouter/qwen/qwen3-coder-plus"). For grok, a Grok model id
#       (default "grok-composer-2.5-fast"; "grok-build" is the reasoning
#       variant). For codex and kimi, omit to use the CLI's own default;
#       set only when you need a specific model the CLI accepts. Ignored
#       for the claude path (the model is chosen by the project's
#       engineer_command wrapper).
#
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Creative-work opt-in (Phase 7 / gs-278). Per-project. Off by default.
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Hard Rule #1 says bots do correctness work; creative work (marketing,
# launch copy, growth strategy) stays with humans. The rule's final
# clause also says creative agents are "opt-in plugins with explicit
# warnings" — these fields implement that opt-in. Full policy context
# in docs/internal/RULE-RELAXATION-2026-04-20.md.
#
# Use with care. Creative cycles have no reviewer gate (there's no
# objective "tests pass" for prose) and no hands_off check on draft
# content — the human-in-the-loop review is the only quality gate.
#
#   creative_work_allowed: bool   (optional, default false)
#       When true AND a picked task has `creative: true`, the cycle
#       proceeds with a CREATIVE_WORK warning, routes outputs to the
#       creative branch, and skips the reviewer. When false/unset,
#       creative-tagged tasks are skipped with reason
#       `creative_work_not_allowed_for_project`.
#
#   creative_work_branch: string   (optional, default "bot/creative-drafts")
#       Separate branch for creative-tagged cycles. Keeps drafts off
#       the main bot/work branch so correctness cycles and creative
#       cycles don't contaminate each other's SHAs.
#
#   creative_work_drafts_dir: string   (optional, default "drafts/")
#       Directory path (relative to project root) where creative
#       outputs land. Makes it easy to .gitignore drafts or review
#       them in isolation.
#
#   voice_reference_paths: string[]   (optional)
#       Files the engineer reads before drafting creative work to
#       calibrate the author's voice. Seed with anything written by
#       the project owner in their actual idiom — manuals, blog
#       posts, prior copy. Order matters: put the highest-signal
#       source first.
#
# Per-task overrides (in tasks.json):
#   creative: true                  # marks this task as creative
#   voice_reference_override: []    # replaces project voice paths
#                                   # for this task specifically
#
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pre-cycle advisor — Hammerstein integration (gs-327, 2026-05-14)
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Optional pre-cycle audit layer. When enabled, GS shells out to the
# `h` CLI (https://github.com/lerugray/hammerstein) between picker
# and engineer with the proposed task plan + bounded cycle history.
# The verdict lands in PROGRESS.jsonl as `advisor_verdict`.
#
# Full docs: docs/ADVISOR.md.
#
#   advisor.enabled: bool   (default: false — zero overhead)
#       When true, advisor runs pre-cycle.
#
#   advisor.gate: bool   (default: false — advisory only)
#       When true AND advisor verdict is "block", the cycle skips
#       (cycle_skipped, reason "advisor_gated"). When false, the
#       verdict is logged but the cycle proceeds regardless.
#
#   advisor.provider: string   (default: "hammerstein")
#       v1 only supports "hammerstein" (uses the `h` CLI). v2+ may
#       add direct OpenRouter/Claude/Ollama routing inside GS.
#
#   advisor.timeout_seconds: int   (default: 90)
#       Wall-clock timeout. `h audit` typically runs ~60s on
#       OpenRouter Qwen3.6-plus. Verdict on timeout: "timeout",
#       cycle proceeds.
#
#   advisor.history_cycles: int   (default: 3)
#       Recent cycles included in the plan context. Bounded per
#       Hammerstein-audit recommendation to prevent advisor
#       context-window dilution.
#
# Latency: ~60s per cycle when enabled. For 10-15 min cycle work,
# acceptable overhead. For 30s mechanical jobs, disable advisor.
#
# Hard Rule 8 (BYOK): every credential above comes from your own
# environment. Nothing is shipped, nothing is hosted on your behalf.

# -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pre-cycle judgment gate — inline Hammerstein slop screen (gs-330, 2026-06-16)
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Optional, lightweight sibling of the advisor above. Instead of shelling
# out to the external `h` CLI, the judgment gate calls OpenRouter INLINE
# with the canonical Hammerstein system-prompt and asks one focused
# question of the picked task: is it LOAD-BEARING toward the project goal,
# or STUPID-INDUSTRIOUS slop (effort that pattern-matches progress but
# doesn't advance it)? Verdict KEEP / REJECT, logged to PROGRESS.jsonl as
# `judgment_verdict`. No external binary required — just OPENROUTER_API_KEY
# (the same key the OpenRouter reviewer path already uses).
#
# Full docs: docs/JUDGMENT-GATE.md.
#
#   judgment_gate: off | flag | skip   (default: off — zero overhead)
#       off  — disabled; the gate never runs.
#       flag — run the gate, log the verdict, PROCEED regardless (advisory).
#       skip — run the gate; on REJECT skip the cycle (cycle_skipped,
#              reason "judged_stupid_industrious"). KEEP / error / no-key
#              all proceed.
#
# Flag-first by design: the Hammerstein framework calls itself "not a veto"
# and the load-bearing-vs-slop boundary is genuinely contestable on
# borderline tasks, so the default never blocks legitimate work. Reach for
# `skip` only on autonomous / auto-generated task pipelines where slop
# sub-goals actually appear — for a hand-written tasks.json it's mostly a
# no-op safety net.
#
# Model: OpenRouter qwen/qwen3.6-plus by default (well under a cent/task;
# override with GENERALSTAFF_JUDGMENT_GATE_MODEL). Latency: ~10-20s/cycle
# when enabled. Composes with `advisor` (both can run). Graceful no-op: a missing
# key, fetch failure, timeout, or unparseable verdict all PROCEED — only an
# explicit REJECT under `skip` blocks a cycle.
#
#   # Recommended dogfood setting for the generalstaff self-managed project
#   # (global default stays off so upgrading users get no surprise calls):
#   judgment_gate: flag
#
# Hard Rule 8 (BYOK): the OpenRouter key comes from your own environment.
# Nothing is shipped, nothing is hosted on your behalf.

# -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Autonomous mode (gs-332, v0.8.0) — OPT-IN, default off
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# GS's autonomous front-end. For each project with `autonomous.enabled`,
# `gs autonomous` SURVEYs its state (MISSION + git log + queued tasks),
# asks an off-cap model to SCOPE concrete next work, runs the Hammerstein
# GATE+CLASSIFY (KEEP/REJECT + BOT-SAFE/DESIGN-FORK), then routes:
# DESIGN-FORK + live-held decisions -> a fork-ledger for you (`gs forks`);
# BOT-SAFE work -> dispatched through the normal cycle (Phase 2). Absent /
# `enabled: false` => the project is never scoped (zero overhead).
#
#   autonomous:
#     enabled: true                   # opt this project in (default false)
#     scoper_model: qwen/qwen3.6-plus # optional; overrides the fleet default
#     scope_count: 3                  # optional; items to propose (default 3)
#     live: false                     # optional; see the PRIVACY note below
#
# PRIVACY — `live: true` marks a REVENUE / live product: even bot-safe work
# is HELD for your review rather than auto-dispatched. WHICH projects are
# revenue products is private data — set `live: true` only in your real
# (gitignored) projects.yaml, never in a publicly-committed file. The
# fork-ledger / dispatch-ledger DATA files are gitignored for the same reason.
#
# BYOK (Hard Rule 8): the scoper + gate run on your OPENROUTER_API_KEY.

# -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Multi-reviewer quorum review (db77c28, 2026-06-02)
# -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Optional. When `review:` is absent (the default), GS uses a single
# reviewer driven by GENERALSTAFF_REVIEWER_PROVIDER — identical to
# all prior releases. Add `review:` with two or more entries to get
# parallel-independent reviewer voices synthesized into one verdict.
# A single-entry `reviewers` list is also valid (backward-compatible
# opt-in without changing the quorum behavior).
#
#   review.reviewers: list   (required when `review:` is present)
#       Each entry is one reviewer voice run independently in
#       parallel. Fields per entry:
#
#       provider: string   (required)
#           One of `claude`, `openrouter`, `ollama`, or any custom
#           name that falls through to the claude path. Same routing
#           space as GENERALSTAFF_REVIEWER_PROVIDER.
#
#       model: string   (optional)
#           Per-reviewer model override. When unset, the provider's
#           own default (or GENERALSTAFF_REVIEWER_MODEL) is used.
#           Voices are threaded per-call so env is never mutated —
#           parallel voices stay genuinely independent.
#
#       fallback: string   (optional)
#           Per-reviewer fallback provider if this voice errors.
#           Same semantics as GENERALSTAFF_REVIEWER_FALLBACK_PROVIDER.
#
#       label: string   (optional)
#           Display label in audit output and PROGRESS.jsonl.
#           Defaults to `provider` (or `provider:model` when model
#           is set).
#
#   review.quorum_policy: "conservative" | "majority"   (optional)
#       How individual reviewer verdicts are synthesized.
#
#       conservative (default): any `verification_failed` from any
#           reviewer holds the merge — safest when `auto_merge: true`.
#           One dissenting voice is sufficient to roll the cycle back.
#
#       majority: pass when more reviewers approve than block. Use
#           when you want the aggregate signal rather than the strictest
#           individual voice (e.g. three reviewers where one tends to
#           over-fire on scope).
#
#   review.min_real_reviews: int   (optional, default: 2)
#       Minimum number of non-errored reviewer responses to count as
#       a genuine quorum. If fewer voices respond without error, GS
#       transparently falls back to single-reviewer behavior and says
#       so in the audit log — it never presents one survivor as if
#       vetted by many. Must be >= 1.
#
# Example — three-voice conservative quorum:
#
#   review:
#     reviewers:
#       - provider: claude
#       - provider: openrouter
#         model: qwen/qwen3-coder-30b-a3b-instruct
#         fallback: claude
#       - provider: ollama
#         model: qwen3:8b
#         label: local-framework-voice
#     quorum_policy: conservative
#     min_real_reviews: 2
#
# No breaking changes. Absent `review:` == today's single-reviewer
# behavior bit-for-bit.

projects:
  - id: example-service
    path: /path/to/example-service
    priority: 1                              # 1 = highest, 5 = lowest
    engineer_command: "bash run_bot.sh ${cycle_budget_minutes}"
    verification_command: >-
      npm test
    # Optional second gate for user-facing work. It runs after the unit gate
    # against the shipped artifact. Unit green != player-path green; for
    # user-facing work only player-path green counts as done.
    # See docs/conventions/player-path-verification.md and the example probe.
    player_path_command: >-
      node scripts/player-path-probe.mjs dist/app.html
      artifacts/player-path.json
    # Optional third gate: claim-vs-screen battery. Runs after player_path
    # (or after unit when player_path is unset). Exit 0 = every enumerated
    # claim judged TRUE against screenshots of the shipped artifact.
    # See docs/conventions/claim-battery.md.
    # claim_battery_command: >-
    #   node scripts/claim-battery.mjs dist/app.html
    #   claims/manifest.json artifacts/claim-battery.json
    cycle_budget_minutes: 60                 # hard timeout per cycle
    work_detection: tasks_json
    concurrency_detection: none
    branch: bot/work
    auto_merge: false                        # MUST be false until 5 clean cycles
    hands_off:
      - secrets/                             # credentials and local secrets
      - product-rules/                       # operator-owned policy
    notes: |
      Generic user-facing example. The project owns its browser adapter and
      coverage map; GeneralStaff runs the authored probe as a gate.

  - id: example-library
    path: /path/to/example-library
    priority: 2
    engineer_command: "<TBD — Phase 3>"
    verification_command: "<TBD — needs test suite before registering>"
    cycle_budget_minutes: 45
    work_detection: tasks_json               # default: GeneralStaff manages tasks
    concurrency_detection: none              # default: no bot infra yet
    branch: bot/work
    auto_merge: false
    hands_off:
      - "<TBD — needs hands_off list before registering>"
    notes: |
      PLACEHOLDER — not registered yet. Pipeline work and sprite
      tests are bot-friendly. Marketing / ideation is
      OFF LIMITS per Hard Rule #1.

  - id: example-prototype
    path: /path/to/example-prototype
    priority: 3
    engineer_command: "<TBD>"
    verification_command: "<TBD>"
    cycle_budget_minutes: 45
    work_detection: tasks_json
    concurrency_detection: none
    branch: bot/work
    auto_merge: false
    hands_off:
      - "<TBD>"
    notes: |
      PLACEHOLDER — not registered yet. Bot work scoped to
      NON-GAME-LOGIC only (UI, scaffolding, save/load, tests).
      Game rules / wargame logic is OFF LIMITS.

# -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dispatcher-level config
# -------------------------------------------------------------------

dispatcher:
  state_dir: ./state                         # per-project state (NEVER in managed projects)
  fleet_state_file: ./fleet_state.json
  stop_file: ./STOP                          # kill switch
  override_file: ./next_project.txt          # picker override
  picker: priority_x_staleness
  max_cycles_per_project_per_session: 3      # Q1: chaining cap
  log_dir: ./logs
  digest_dir: ./digests

  # -----------------------------------------------------------------
  # Parallel mode — Phase 4 opt-in (gs-186)
  # -----------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Run N cycles in parallel per round. Default 1 preserves the
  # sequential dispatcher from Phases 1-3 (chaining, STOP mid-cycle,
  # no concurrent reviewer calls) bit-for-bit.
  #
  # When to turn it on: your fleet has 2+ projects with real
  # backlogs each AND session wall-clock is the bottleneck. Round-
  # based strict-wait — all slots in a round finish before the next
  # round starts. Chaining is disabled in parallel mode (each round
  # picks fresh projects); an empty-queue project gets skipped
  # rather than padded into an idle slot (gs-232).
  #
  # Cost: parallel N roughly multiplies reviewer-step API spend by
  # N on external providers. Hard Rule 8 (BYOK) applies — the cost
  # shows up on your bill. Per-provider semaphores (default
  # claude=∞, openrouter=2, ollama=1) prevent reviewer stampedes;
  # tune via GENERALSTAFF_REVIEWER_CONCURRENCY_<PROVIDER>.
  #
  # Recommended: start at 2 on a 3-project fleet, watch
  # `slot_idle_seconds` in session_complete + `status --sessions`,
  # bump only if utilization stays high. Drop back to 1 if
  # reviewer-spend or UX feedback warrants.
  max_parallel_slots: 1

  # Autonomous-mode fleet defaults (gs-332, v0.8.0). Per-project
  # `autonomous:` fields override these. NOTE: unlike most dispatcher
  # fields (which silently default on bad input), a MALFORMED autonomous
  # block here THROWS at config load — a misconfigured scoper/cap should
  # be loud, not silently ignored (same posture as session_budget).
  # autonomous:
  #   scoper_model: qwen/qwen3.6-plus  # fleet default scoper
  #   scope_count: 3                   # default items proposed per project
  #   dispatch_cap: 2                  # Phase 2: non-live dispatches per run
  #   live_dispatch: false             # Phase 2: also branch-dispatch live products
  #   live_dispatch_cap: 1             # Phase 2: conservative live cap
